PASSWD(5)                      P32W Unix                       PASSWD(5)

NAME
       passwd - password file

SYNOPSIS
       #include <pwd.h>

DESCRIPTION
       /etc/passwd lists the users, one line each, seven fields
       separated by colons:

              name:password:uid:gid:comment:home:shell

       name       the login name
       password   the encrypted password (below)
       uid        the numeric user id; 0 is the super-user
       gid        the numeric group id of the login group (group(5))
       comment    usually the full name
       home       the home directory: login changes to it and sets
                  $HOME
       shell      the program login runs; empty means /bin/sh

       Lines that are empty or start with # are skipped. The file
       as shipped:

              root:1a2b$44337a05b15e80e5:0:0:Super-user:/:/bin/sh
              ken:3c4d$5e9b91a6ac61e0fc:100:10:Ken:/usr/ken:/bin/sh
              guest::200:20:Guest:/usr/guest:/bin/sh

       The password field is one of

       (empty)    no password: login does not ask for one.
       *          no login: no password matches.
       SSSS$HHHHHHHHHHHHHHHH
                  a hashed password: 4 hex digits of salt, a $, and
                  16 hex digits of hash, 21 characters in all.

       Any other value matches no password. The hash is computed
       by pwhash(password, salt) in the C library: two 32-bit
       lanes a and b start as

              a = 0x811C9DC5 ^ s        b = 0x9E3779B9 ^ (s * 0x10001)

       where s is the 16-bit salt; then 2000 rounds r = 0..1999 each
       mix in every byte c of the password,

              a = (a ^ c) * 0x01000193
              b = rotl(b ^ a, 5) * 0x9E3779B1

       and end with

              a ^= rotl(b, 13) + r
              b += a ^ 0x5BD1E995

       (all arithmetic modulo 2^32). The result is printed as
       "%04x$%08x%08x" of s, a and b. To check a password, login(1)
       and su(1) hash what was typed with the stored salt and
       compare. passwd(1) makes a new salt from the time and the
       process id. On the host, tools/mkpasswd.py PASSWORD [SALT]
       computes the same hash, for building a disk image.

       The hash is deliberately slow, but it is not a defence
       against someone who can read the disk: /etc/passwd is
       readable by everyone.

FILES
       /etc/passwd
       /etc/ptmp      passwd(1)'s temporary copy

SEE ALSO
       login(1), passwd(1), su(1), adduser(8), group(5),
       getpwent(3), id(1)

BUGS
       There is no shadow file. Lines longer than 255 characters
       are cut short. passwd(1) and adduser(8) do not lock the
       file. Only the first 63 characters of a password are read.
